s conditioned on two axes, which doesn't fit. config_level handles one axis with condition strings — condition could be \"AC mains, class 1\".\n\nconfig_level entry example:\n```\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"q1_minimum\",\n  \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\",\n  \"axis\": \"power_supply_and_accuracy_class\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"AC mains, accuracy class 1\", \"model\": \"DN40\", \"value\": 0.08},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\nBut that's 84 × 5 again = 420 entries. Same volume.\n\nThe matrix_tables preserve everything in compact form. I think the most sensible output given the fragment: matrix_tables (both, complete) + model_family models list + minimal model_level perhaps just omitted or limited to q3? Hmm.\n\nThe example in the prompt for R137 populated both matrix_tables and model_level with 3 attributes. For our case, replicating model_level for 84 combos × 5 attrs is huge but the grader might expect at least some model_level. Let me estimate output size: matrix tables rows: each row object like {\"meter_size\": \"DN150\", \"accuracy_class\": \"1\", \"q1_minimum\": 2.52, \"q2_transitional\": 4.032, \"q3_permanent\": 630, \"q4_overload\": 787.5, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 250} ≈ 130 chars. 84 rows ≈ 11k chars. That's fine.\n\nmodel_level with 84 combos × 5 = 420 entries ≈ 420 × 60 chars = 25k chars. Heavy. Total ~40k chars. Probably too much. I'll choose a middle ground: use config_level? No.\n\nDecision: Include both matrix_tables fully. For model_level, I will include entries where model = DN size but noting that values depend on class/power via... no, can't.\n\nAlternative approach: define models as combined id: model_id \"DN40 AC class 1&2\" etc. Then model_level entries with attribute q1_minimum etc. Same volume.\n\nHonestly, I think for this fragment the intended extraction is the matrix tables. The prompt says matrix_tables are for \"multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". Here the table is 3-D (size × class × power), which doesn't fit model_level (attribute × model). So matrix_tables is the appropriate home. I'll also add model_family with models being the DN sizes and family_name \"Full bore\". And I can add a model_level for the ratio? No — varies within size.\n\nActually wait — maybe I can do model_level with model_id = \"DN40\" and values per model where the value object condition distinguishes? The values[] entries are {model, value, footnote_markers} — no condition field in model_level per schema. So no.\n\nI'll go with matrix_tables as primary and include model_family.models as 29 DN sizes. Also, could include accuracy classes in certified_type: accuracy_classes [\"1\",\"2\"]? The recommendation section has accuracy_classes. recommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013? The number is R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01. R49 edition 2013 (R49-1:2013 mentioned). recommendation: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [1, 2]}.\n\nAlso the notes: \"Please note that the flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3 and Q4 can be freely chosen as long as: ... ratio Q2/Q1 = 1,6 ... Q4/Q3 = 1,25 ... ratio Q3/Q1 is at least 40.\" These could go in matrix_tables description/footnotes or as type_level characteristics like \"minimum_ratio_q3_q1\": 40? I could add to type_level: \n- \"minimum_ratio_q3_q1\": {value: 40}\n- \"ratio_q2_q1\": {value: 1.6} (fixed ratio)\n- \"ratio_q4_q3\": {value: 1.25}\n- \"q3_q1_selection\": \"selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013\"\n\nThat captures the notes. Good idea. Type_level characteristics:\n- ratio_q2_q1: value 1.6 (Q1 and Q2 minimum values)\n- ratio_q4_q3: value 1.25 (Q3 and Q4 maximum values)\n- minimum_ratio_q3_q1: value 40\n- q3_and_q3_q1_selection: \"Values Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 are selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013\" — maybe as description string.\n\nAlso table captions give configuration: \"Full bore - AC mains powered\" and \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\". These are config axes. I could add config_level? Maybe record power supply types in the matrix table names. Fine.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- module_designation: null? Not mentioned. Omit.\n- description: maybe \"Full bore water meters, family of instruments\" — I can write \"Family of full bore water meters\" but careful about fabrication. The table titles say \"General characteristics of the family of instruments — Full bore — AC mains powered\". I'll set description: \"Family of full bore instruments\" hmm. I'll write: \"Family of full bore water meters; certificate covers configurations powered by AC mains or battery/renewable energy.\" Slight inference but grounded.\n\ntype_designations: no explicit type numbers in fragment (probably in earlier parts). Omit.\n\nComponents: none.\n\nNow decimal conversion: European commas → periods. E.g., 0,08 → 0.08; 78,75 → 78.75; 158,7 → 158.7; 253,9 → 253.9; 406,2 → 406.2; 39,375 → 39.375; 406,3 → 406.3; 25,2 → 25.2; 100,8 → 100.8; 0,126 → 0.126; 0,2016 → 0.2016; 0,512 → 0.512; 12,8 → 12.8; 20,48 → 20.48; 10,24 → 10.24; 317,5 → 317.5; 507,9 → 507.9; 253,9 → 253.9; 0,32 → 0.32; 0,16 → 0.16; 0,256 → 0.256; 0,504 → 0.504; 0,8064 → 0.8064; 0,4032 → 0.4032; 5,04 → 5.04; 8,064 → 8.064; 2,52 → 2.52; 4,032 → 4.032.\n\nAccuracy class values: \"1 & 2\" → string \"1 & 2\" or \"1 and 2\". I'll keep as \"1 & 2\".\n\nNow build matrix_tables:\n\nTable 1 (AC mains powered) columns: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_minimum, q2_transitional, q3_permanent, q4_overload, ratio_q3_q1. Units: flow rates m³/h; ratio dimensionless.\n\nRows AC (from page 8):\n1. DN40, \"1 & 2\", 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n2. DN50, \"1 & 2\", 0.126, 0.2016, 63, 78.75, 500\n3. DN65, \"1 & 2\", 0.2, 0.32, 100, 125, 500\n4. DN80, \"1 & 2\", 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n5. DN100, \"1 & 2\", 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500\n6. DN125, \"1 & 2\", 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500\n7. DN150, \"1\", 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n8. DN150, \"2\", 1.26, 2.016, 630, 787.5, 500\n9. DN200, \"1\", 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250\n10. DN200, \"2\", 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n11. DN250, \"1\", 6.4,