y\"]}}. That adds info. But careful — DN40 AC appears with classes \"1 & 2\"; battery with classes 1 and 2 separately. Keep it simple: model_id list only, maybe with attributes for available power options. I'll add attributes: {\"power_supply_options\": [...]}.\n\nActually, maybe simpler and safer: models = [{\"model_id\": \"DN40\"}, ...] with attributes only where informative. I'll include power options since it's derivable directly from table captions. Eh, it's fine either way. I'll include it — it preserves info about which sizes are in which config. Actually the matrix_tables already encode that via rows. Duplication acceptable.\n\nI'll keep models simple: model_id + label + attributes {power_supply: [...]}.\n\nNow should I add model_level characteristics? Given tables already carry the data and it's 3-dimensional, I'll add a small model_level set only where a single value holds for the whole DN size across classes/configs? Check: DN100: AC Q3 250 (both classes), battery Q3 250 (1&2). So q3_permanent for DN100 = 250 in both configs. DN125: same, 250. DN350: AC 4000, battery 4000. DN400: 4000 both. DN450: 6300 both. DN500: 6300 both. DN600: 6300 both (battery class2 6300 yes). DN700: 10000 both. DN750: 10000. DN800: 10000. DN900: 10000. DN1000: 16000. DN1050: 16000. DN1100: 16000. DN1200: 16000. DN1350: 16000. DN1400: 16000. DN1500: 16000. DN1600: 16000. DN1650: 16000. DN1800: 16000 (AC only). DN40: 40 both. DN50: 63 both. DN65: 100 both. DN80: 160 both. DN150: 630 both. DN200: 1000 both. DN250: 1600 both. DN300: 2500 both.\n\nInteresting — Q3 is constant per DN size across classes and power configs! Q3 depends only on meter size. So I can emit model_level for q3_permanent: 29 entries. Also Q4 = 1.25 × Q3, constant per size too: DN40 50, DN50 78.75, DN65 125, DN80 200, DN100 312.5, DN125 312.5, DN150 787.5, DN200 1250, DN250 2000, DN300 3125, DN350 5000, DN400 5000, DN450 7875, DN500 7875, DN600 7875, DN700 12500, DN750 12500, DN800 12500, DN900 12500, DN1000 20000, DN1050 20000, DN1100 20000, DN1200 20000, DN1350 20000, DN1400 20000, DN1500 20000, DN1600 20000, DN1650 20000, DN1800 20000. Yes constant per size.\n\nQ1 and Q2 vary by class and power config, so they stay in matrix tables only. Ratio varies too.\n\nSo model_level: q3_permanent and q4_overload per model (29 entries each). That's a nice middle ground consistent with the schema and not too verbose. And model_level values: include footnote? None.\n\nHmm, but is Q3 truly constant per size? DN900 battery: Q3 = 10000 (class 2 row), AC class1 10000. Yes. DN1000 battery 16000, AC 16000. Yes.\n\nGood. So:\nmodel_level:\n- {attribute: \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\", unit_symbol: \"m³/h\", values: [{model: \"DN40\", value: 40}, ...29]}\n- {attribute: \"overload_flow_rate_q4\", unit_symbol: \"m³/h\", values: [...]}\n\nAlso could add q3_q1_ratio range? Varies; skip.\n\nType-level characteristics from the notes:\n- \"ratio_q2_q1\": {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: null} — from \"the ratio Q2/Q1 = 1,6\" (Q1, Q2 minimum values note).\n- \"ratio_q4_q3\": {value: 1.25}\n- \"minimum_ratio_q3_q1\": {value: {min: 40, max: null}} — \"The ratio Q3/Q1 is at least 40\" → {min: 40, max: null}.\n- \"flow_rate_selection_reference\": value \"OIML R49-1:2013 paragraph 4.1\" — maybe as note. Could include as characteristic \"q3_and_ratio_selection\": \"Values Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013\". I'll include one such string characteristic: {\"flow_rate_selection\": {\"value\": \"Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013; Q1 and Q2 are minimum values; Q3 and Q4 are maximum values\"}}. Hmm, maybe simpler to put these notes into matrix_tables description and footnotes. I'll do both: put description on each table and add type_level values for ratios.\n\nCertificate fields: number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\", scheme \"A\", project_number \"3891281\", page_total 13, member_state \"The Netherlands\". Pages in fragment: 8–10. The schema doesn't have a page field... \"page_total\" only. I might add \"pages_in_fragment\": [8,9,10]? Not in schema. I'll add \"revision\": \"11\" — extra field but valuable. Actually, let me reconsider: the schema explicitly lists certificate fields. Adding extra keys risks schema validation failure if strict. The safest is to append revision to number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 11\"? That pollutes the number for dedup matching. Common approach in prior examples of this task family: number without revision, and revision_history[] with revision \"11\" but date/changes unknown — revision_history requires date and changes. Rule 8: don't fabricate. Hmm.\n\nI'll go with certificate.number = \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and add \"revision\": \"11\" as an extra key. Extra keys are typically tolerated in these tasks. Actually, let me think about risk: the instruction \"produce a JSON object following the layered schema\" — extra key \"revision\" is a minor extension, and the info is present in the doc. I'll include it.\n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi\". address not in fragment; omit address_lines. Actually, maybe include issuing_authority with just name. NMi (Netherlands Measuring institute). I'll include {\"name\": \"NMi\"}.\n\nRecommendation: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"]}. accuracy_classes as numbers or strings? Example doesn't show. I'll use [1, 2] numbers... or strings \"1\",\"2\". The table says accuracy class 1 & 2. I'll use [\"1\",\"2\"] strings? Hmm — accuracy classes are typically \"1\" and \"2\". JSON numbers are fine. I'll use [1, 2].\n\nWait, recommendation.scheme — in \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\", the A indicates scheme A. certificate.scheme: \"A\". Include in both.\n\nDate issued: not in fragment. Omit.\n\nNow certified_type: category \"Water meter\". type_designations: none present → omit. description: \"Full bore water meters; family of instruments\" — I'll write: \"Family of full bore instruments (water meters), AC mains powered or battery / renewable energy powered configurations\". Slight paraphrase, grounded in captions. OK.\n\nmodule_designation: null — omit or null? Schema shows it can be null. I'll omit.\n\nAlso maybe \"config_level\" for power supply axis: axis \"power_supply\", conditions \"Full bore - AC mains powered\" / \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\" — but the attribute values are the tables themselves; config_level expects attribute values. I'll skip config_level; the two matrix tables capture it.\n\nmatrix_tables schema fields: name, columns[], rows[], description, footnotes[]. The R137 example also had \"column_units\". I'll include column_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null].\n\nColumns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\", \"transitional_flow_rate_q2\", \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\", \"overload_flow_rate_q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"].\n\nNames for tables: \"Table 7 General characteristics of the family of instruments — Full bore - AC mains powered\" and \"Table 8 General characteristics of the family of instruments — Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\".\n\nDescription for each: \"Flow rates Q1 and Q2 are minimum values (Q2/Q1 = 1.6); Q3 and Q4 are maximum values (Q4/Q3 = 1.25). Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013; ratio Q3/Q1 at least 40. Flow rates can be freely chosen within these constraints.\" Plus for table 1: pages 8–9; table 2: pages 9–10.\n\nFootnotes: none — the notes are block text, not numbered footnotes. I could put the notes in footnotes[] of the tables? They aren't footnotes. Put in description. Good.\n\nNow also the \"Power\" configuration: for table 1 caption \"Full bore - AC mains powered\" — I'll name: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments — Full bore, AC mains powered\".\n\nmodel_family.family_name: \"Full bore\". \n\nLet me now also double check DN900 battery: \"158,7\" → 158.7, Q2 \"254\" → 254 (note: AC table had 253.9 for class1 and 128 for class2; battery DN900 class2: Q1 158.7, Q2 254). And DN1100 battery class 2: Q1 254, Q2 406.3. AC DN1100 class 1: 253.9, 406.2. OK, transcription as-is.\n\nDN1000 battery: Q2 250 (not 256). Yes, table says 250. Keep as printed.\n\nNow accuracy class values \"1 & 2\" — keep as string \"1 & 2\".\n\nmodel_level q3 values:\nDN40 40, DN50 63, DN65 100, DN80 160, DN100 250, DN125 250, DN150 630, DN200 1000, DN250 1600, DN300 2500, DN350 4000, DN400 4000, DN450 6300, DN500 6300, DN600 6300, DN700 10000, DN750 10000, DN800 10000, DN900 10000, DN1000 16000, DN1050 16000, DN1100 16000, DN1200 16000, DN1350 16000, DN1400 16000, DN1500 16000, DN1600 16000, DN1650 16000, DN1800 16000.\n\nq4 values: DN40 50, DN50 78.75, DN65 125, DN80 200, DN100 312.5, DN125 312.5, DN150 787.5, DN200 1250, DN250 2000, DN300 3125, DN350 5000, DN400 5000, DN450 7875, DN500 7875, DN600 7875, DN700 12500, DN750 12500, DN800 12500, DN900 12500, DN1000 20000, DN1050 20000, DN1100 20000, DN1200 20000, DN1350 20000, DN1400 20000, DN1500 20000, DN1600 20000, DN1650 20000, DN1800 20000.\n\nCheck DN900 battery Q4 = 12500 yes; DN900 AC class1 Q4 12500. DN1000 battery Q4 20000 yes.\n\nAlso note: q4_overload is max value; q1/q2 min. Should I mark these as max/min? The attribute names include minimum/maximum semantics: \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\", \"overload_flow_rate_q4\". Fine.\n\nAttribute naming for model_level: \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\" and \"overload_flow_rate_q4\". Keep consistent with matrix column names.\n\nDo values need structured form in model_level? Schema rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value/unit_symbol/etc. But the R137 example shows model_level values as {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} without unit object — the unit is at the attribute level (\"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"). So model_level values entries: {model, value, footnote_markers?}. I'll follow the example: value numeric, unit at attribute level.\n\ntype_level structured: \n- ratio_q2_q1: {\"value\": 1.6,